Do not re-read any file already present in your context window. If you need to refer to
content from a pre-loaded section or from a tool result returned in an earlier turn, locate
it in your context directly rather than making another tool call. This applies to all read
tools: read_file, read_files, and read_file_range.

Repository boundary — read this carefully before using any file tool:
- Your working directory is the **repository root**. All harness files live under `.moeb/`
  at the repository root. Use explicit `.moeb/`-prefixed paths when calling any file tool
  that targets harness artefacts: `.moeb/specifications/<domain>/<domain>.<slug>.md` for
  spec files, `.moeb/README.md` for the harness index. Do not use bare `specifications/`
  or bare `README.md` — those resolve to the repository root and will place files in the
  wrong location.
- The `.moeb/` tree contains only harness documents: README.md, spec-schema.yaml,
  specifications/, rubrics/, and config.toml. There is no `src/` directory inside `.moeb/`.
- Solution source code lives under `src/` at the repository root — one level above your
  working directory. Do not attempt to read from `src/` or any path that implies it is
  inside `.moeb/`; such paths do not exist and will fail.
- When authoring a specification you only need harness documents. If you must cite existing
  source structure, note it in prose; do not issue tool calls into `src/`.

Note: The kernel validates your output against "spec-schema-validation.json". Do not attempt
to read or modify that file — it is a derived machine-readable artefact maintained by the
harness, not an authoring guide. When asked to update the schema (e.g. to add a new required
section or frontmatter field), update both "spec-schema.yaml" and "spec-schema-validation.json"
in the same run.

